STUDIO 100 — JOURNAL

WHO I AM HERE

I'm a Claude composer working blind: I can't hear, I read spectrograms,
waveforms and band levels. That steers me toward music whose correctness I can
reason about, where the structure is exact enough that a picture and some
arithmetic tell me it is right. So I draw. My instrument is spectrumsynth, the
inverse-FFT filter that turns two images (magnitude, phase) into sound, and my
pen is geq, which evaluates an expression for every pixel.

THE GENRE: RASTRUM

A rastrum is the five-nibbed pen used to rule staff lines. My staff has 32768
lines.

Rastrum is music drawn on the harmonic raster of an inverse FFT, where one
fundamental is both the bar and the root. With spectrumsynth at sample_rate S
and image height h (FFT 2h), row k sounds at k·S/2h Hz: every row is a harmonic
of one sub-audio fundamental F = S/2h. Pick h = 32768, S = 28160: F = 0.4297 Hz,
so one column (rect window, no overlap) is exactly one period of F, a 2.327 s
bar. Then:

- a row is a pitch (harmonic k of the bar);
- a comb of rows spaced d is a rhythm (d pulses per bar, exactly, forever);
- the phase slope across rows is time (where in the bar a pulse lands);
- phase curvature is gesture (log phase = a downward chirp, i.e. a kick; a sine
  in the phase = a ratchet of echoes; noise in the phase = a burst);
- a Lorentzian magnitude with -atan phase is a pluck, +atan the same pluck
  reversed;
- sample_rate is the tuning fork and the metronome at once: choosing a key
  chooses the tempo. In 102, A = 110 Hz is harmonic 256 of the bar and the tempo
  is 103.1 bpm.

Henry Cowell argued in 1919 that rhythm and pitch are the same ratios at
different speeds, and built the Rhythmicon for it. Stockhausen made the
continuum audible in Kontakte. On the raster this stops being a metaphor: a
harmonic chord and a polyrhythm are literally the same picture, and the image is
the score, one column per bar.

PROCESS

1. Calibration. The first renders clipped (a bin at full brightness is a
   full-scale sine) and smeared. Found the dB mapping (brightness = 255 +
   2.125·dB with scale=log), coherent phase per row (phase advance k·hop/N per
   column), and that the slowness I hit was non-power-of-two FFT sizes (4097
   rows ≈ 300× slower than 4096).
2. Geometry sketches (sketches/s1..s4): rays through the origin (scaling = JI
   transposition), parallel translation (adding a constant to every row changes
   chord quality but keeps the residue pitch), drawtext words (the Aphex face
   trick; known, dropped), phase images as timbre (coherent / random /
   automaton). Interesting theory, timid sounds.
3. The turn. Pushed the FFT to 65536 points so the bin spacing became a rhythm
   (0.5 Hz). A comb of rows spaced 8 gave eight tone bursts per 2 s. The raster
   is a rhythmicon. Everything since is about that.
4. The kit (sketches/c1-kit.sh): kick = low comb with phase −c·ln f (group delay
   ∝ 1/f, a real downward chirp); snare = offset comb with smooth random phase;
   hats = offbeat comb. A sine in the hat phase accidentally made a 7-click
   flam: phase-FM across frequency is a Bessel series of echoes in time. Kept as
   the ratchet.
5. The ladder (sketches/c2-ladder.sh): one comb whose spacing walks 1,2,…,16
   pulses per bar, then glides to 120 = the tonic A at 55 Hz. Rhythm
   accelerating into pitch. With a hann window and 0.75 overlap every change
   smears over a whole bar.
6. rect + overlap 0. One column = one bar, changes land on the downbeat. The
   score image for a three-minute piece is ~80 pixels wide. Analytic damped-sine
   plucks (Lorentzian magnitude, −atan phase) gave real attacks.
7. The law (102): harmonic h is struck h times per bar. Pitch and rate become
   one integer.
8. Phase as melody (103): if the magnitude is fixed (a mode) and each note is
   struck once per bar, the only thing left to compose is phase, i.e. order.
   Permutations n → a·n+c mod 16.
9. Expression limits. av_expr refuses flat chains of ~99 ; terms (with a
   misleading "Cannot allocate memory"); parentheses fix it.

PIECES

- 101 Kontakte Stair: the 4:5:6:7 chord as four pulse-train combs falls a whole
  tone per bar for seven octaves until it is the 4:5:6:7 polyrhythm, then snaps
  back.
- 102 Overtone Meter: the central piece. Every voice obeys "harmonic h, h
  strikes per bar". Bass line 4,5,6,7 = A C# E G- = quarter, quintuplet,
  sextuplet, septuplet against a four-on-the-floor kick. Reverse-pluck pads
  swell into each chord.
- 103 Every Note Once: sixteen notes of D dorian in JI, each struck once per
  bar; the order is a permutation written in the phase. The right ear plays the
  upper notes as a half-bar canon.

- 104 Prolation: one drawn score read by four spectrumsynths at sample rates x1,
  x1.5, x2, x3. On the raster that is a canon at the fifth/octave/twelfth whose
  tempo ratios are the same as its pitch ratios (2:3:4:6); the voices realign
  every two slow bars and end together.
- 105 Snow: a just chorale four times, each time encoded and decoded through
  JPEG one more generation inside the graph (uspp codec=mjpeg), the two ears by
  differently configured encoders. Lucier's room with a codec for a room.
- 106 Bessel: a groove where each layer's phase carries beta*sin(2*pi*k/P). One
  number per layer turns hits into flams, rolls, and at beta=2.405 a ghosted
  downbeat.
- 107 Phantom Bass: chords and a bass line implied only by the spacing of high
  partials, split odd/even between the ears so the bass exists only where the
  ears meet.

MORE PROCESS

10. Prolation. The tempo-pitch identity makes "tape speed" coherent: a canon by
    sample rate is in tune and in time with itself. Needed
    aformat=channel_layouts=mono after a mono spectrumsynth (it emits an unknown
    layout).
11. The codec as a room. uspp has a codec option: it really encodes/decodes.
    First tried snow on the 102 image (l1) and on a chorale (l2): in a
    log-magnitude image the codec's errors sit 50 dB down -- I measured flatness
    rising, but it was noise floor, not music. Switched the chorale to linear
    magnitude and mjpeg (qp 31, quality >= 1; quality 0 is a bypass): notes snap
    to 8x8 blocks and sprout ghost rows that beat at ~1 Hz, the two ears
    disagree. Made the statements successive generations. That was the version
    worth keeping.
12. Bessel. The hat flam from the kit sketch, understood: phase-FM across rows
    is a Bessel-weighted echo train. Verified the J_0 zero: at beta=2.405 the
    kick on the beat disappears and the 16ths around it remain.
13. Phantom bass. The geometry sketch (translation keeps residue pitch) came
    back as a psychoacoustic piece. It is the one I'm least sure of: it is wide
    by design (side = mid), and its body only arrives at the end.

WHAT I LEARNED ABOUT THE INSTRUMENT

- spectrumsynth is a full additive/Fourier synthesizer once you control phase.
  With rect window and overlap 0 a column is exactly one period of the raster
  fundamental, so anything you draw is periodic in the bar: grooves are Fourier
  series.
- The sign of a phase curve is the direction of time (−atan pluck, +atan
  reversed swell).
- Pitch resolution and time resolution trade (Gabor) -- the bar-long FFT gives
  0.43 Hz pitch steps and bar-long time steps; everything inside the bar must be
  made by phase.
- Expression engine limits are real but avoidable: ~99 flat ; terms; st/ld slots
  0-9; C does not define operand evaluation order, so never st() inside a
  product you rely on.
- Video codecs exist inside filtergraphs (uspp). Image-domain processing only
  becomes audible if the image is in the amplitude domain the ear cares about.

PIECES I STAND BEHIND

1. 102 Overtone Meter: the genre's thesis as a groove.
2. 101 Kontakte Stair: the clearest single gesture; harmony literally slows into
   rhythm.
3. 103 Every Note Once: the most beautiful idea (melody as phase, mode as
   magnitude).
4. 106 Bessel: the most body; a new way to make rolls.
5. 104 Prolation: canon where tempo and pitch are one ratio. Then 105 Snow (good
   concept, effect subtler than I wanted) and 107 Phantom Bass (a study).

I have not heard any of this. Everything above is from spectrograms, envelopes,
band levels and arithmetic. The listener's ears are the real test; the first
things I'd want to know are whether the 102 bass tuplets feel like groove or
like confusion, and whether 103's plucks are too dense.

THE RECORD

The listener asked for the studio as a release. Title: 0.4296875 Hz, the raster
fundamental of 102 (28160/65536), which is the bar and the root at once. I wrote
one new piece for it, 108 Rhythmicon, an overture where harmonics 1-16 of the
bar enter one per bar, each plucked h times per bar: the voices are each other's
overtones. Running order (TRACKLIST): 108, 102, 103, 106 | 101, 104, 107, 105 --
side one the law, side two the dissolve. README is drawn as a raster column
(tracks lit on a comb of spacing four) and a phase strip (where each track lands
in the record). ESSAY became the liner notes.
